  "decision": "OPEN",
  "incumbent_segment": "Sequential search of visible receptacles in observation order (cabinet_1 then cabinet_2) to locate and acquire the target object.",
  "evidence_status": {
    "feasibility_support": "The trajectory successfully acquired the saltshaker by checking cabinet_1, finding no match, then checking cabinet_2, opening it, and taking the item. This confirms the listed-order search strategy is feasible for this instance.",
    "comparative_support": "No alternative search strategies (e.g., prioritizing likely locations like drawers or shelves based on object type priors, or random exploration) were tested or compared against this sequential approach. The memory establishes the behavior works but does not prove it is optimal or preferred over other valid search orders.",
    "policy_relevance": "Search efficiency directly impacts step count and potential reward in ALFWorld tasks. If a different heuristic (e.g., 'saltshakers are often in drawers') yields faster acquisition, adopting that policy would materially improve performance across similar pick-and-place tasks."
  },
  "functional_contract": {
    "available_state": "Agent at start location with knowledge of all visible receptacles; target object hidden in one of them.",
    "local_function": "Iterate through candidate receptacles according to a specific ordering policy until the target is found and taken.",
    "required_downstream_state": "Agent holding the target object, ready for transport/placement phase.",
    "constraints": [
      "Must only visit admissible locations.",
      "Must open closed containers before inspection.",
      "Must stop searching once target is acquired."
    ]
  },
  "warrant": "The incumbent uses a naive sequential search order derived from the initial observation list. While effective here, it lacks comparative evidence against more intelligent heuristics (e.g., object-location priors). Since search cost is a primary driver of task difficulty in ALFWorld, determining if a better ordering exists is policy-relevant."
